issue_context.body:
### Description

Through .NET 8.0, `System.String.IsInterned(System.String)` returned either the same string passed in to it (if that string was interned) or `null` (if the string was not interned). This matches the [documentation for this method](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.string.isinterned?view=net-8.0). The implementation for .NET 8.0 is:

```
public static string? IsInterned(string str)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(str, "str");
    return str.IsInterned();
}
```

As of .NET 9.0 Preview 1, `IsInterned` now always interns the string passed in to it. Its implementation is:

```
public static string? IsInterned(string str)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(str, "str");
    Intern(new StringHandleOnStack(ref str));
    return str;
}
```

This appears to be an unintentional change in behavior while converting FCalls to QCalls in #95670.

### Reproduction Steps

When run under .NET Framework or versions of .NET [Core] up to 8.0, this snippet indicates that the string is not interned (as expected). When run under .NET 9.0 Preview 1, this snippet indicates that the string _is_ interned (because it just interned it).

```
string value = Guid.NewGuid().ToString();

Console.WriteLine($"Interned == {ReferenceEquals(value, string.IsInterned(value))}");
```

[StringInterningRepro.zip](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/files/14517340/StringInterningRepro.zip)

### Expected behavior

I do not expect `System.String.IsInterned` to intern the string passed in to it.

### Actual behavior

`System.String.IsInterned` interns the string passed in to it.

### Regression?

This behavior is new with .NET 9.0 Preview 1. It was not seen with .NET Framework or any prior .NET [Core] versions.

### Known Workarounds

_No response_

### Configuration

- .NET 9.0 Preview 1
- Windows 11
- x64

